In a DDoS attack, multiple sources of traffic are used to overwhelm the target system. These sources can be from different locations around the world, and they can use various types of traffic, such as HTTP requests, DNS requests, or even malicious code. The goal is to make the target system so overwhelmed that it cannot handle the traffic and becomes unavailable to legitimate users.
DDoS attacks can be difficult to detect and defend against, as they often use techniques to evade detection and make it difficult for defenders to identify the source of the attack. Some common techniques used in DDoS attacks include using proxy servers, rotating IP addresses, and using techniques to mask the source of the traffic.
